 "sq": [
  {
   "_id": 9365,
   "ANSWER": "<p>Machine-level code allows direct access to registers and memory, resulting in optimized performance and smaller footprint critical for hardware-level operations.</p>\n",
   "COURSE_CODE": "21CSC203P",
   "LEVEL": 2,
   "OUTCOMES": [
    "PLO1",
    "PLO2"
   ],
   "QUESTION_DESC": "<p>An embedded system requires precise control over hardware. Why might machine-level programming be preferred over high-level languages?</p>\n",
   "SESSION_ID": 109,
   "TYPE": "sq",
   "key": 9365
  },
  {
   "_id": 9366,
   "ANSWER": "<p>High-level languages abstract hardware details, offering built-in libraries, error handling, and memory management, thus simplifying development and enhancing compatibility.</p>\n",
   "COURSE_CODE": "21CSC203P",
   "LEVEL": 2,
   "OUTCOMES": [
    "PLO1",
    "PLO2"
   ],
   "QUESTION_DESC": "<p>A cross-platform application is being developed with portability in mind. What advantage does a high-level language offer in this context?</p>\n",
   "SESSION_ID": 109,
   "TYPE": "sq",
   "key": 9366
  }
 ],
 "lq": [
  {
   "_id": 9382,
   "ANSWER": "<table>\n\t<thead>\n\t\t<tr>\n\t\t\t<th>Feature</th>\n\t\t\t<th>Machine Code</th>\n\t\t\t<th>C Language</th>\n\t\t</tr>\n\t</thead>\n\t<tbody>\n\t\t<tr>\n\t\t\t<td>Efficiency</td>\n\t\t\t<td>High, no abstraction</td>\n\t\t\t<td>Slightly less due to abstraction</td>\n\t\t</tr>\n\t\t<tr>\n\t\t\t<td>Readability</td>\n\t\t\t<td>Poor, not human-friendly</td>\n\t\t\t<td>High, uses meaningful syntax</td>\n\t\t</tr>\n\t\t<tr>\n\t\t\t<td>Control</td>\n\t\t\t<td>Maximum hardware control</td>\n\t\t\t<td>Provides low-level memory access</td>\n\t\t</tr>\n\t</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<ul data-end=\"6456\" data-start=\"6229\">\n\t<li data-end=\"6322\" data-start=\"6229\">\n\t<p data-end=\"6322\" data-start=\"6231\"><strong data-end=\"6247\" data-start=\"6231\">Machine code</strong> provides direct access to hardware but is difficult to write and maintain.</p>\n\t</li>\n\t<li data-end=\"6456\" data-start=\"6323\">\n\t<p data-end=\"6456\" data-start=\"6325\"><strong data-end=\"6339\" data-start=\"6325\">C language</strong> balances control with readability, allowing low-level access using pointers, bit manipulation, and memory-mapped IO.</p>\n\t</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p data-end=\"6549\" data-start=\"6458\">For embedded systems, C is typically preferred, with <strong data-end=\"6530\" data-start=\"6511\">inline assembly</strong> used where needed.</p>\n",
   "COURSE_CODE": "21CSC203P",
   "LEVEL": 3,
   "OUTCOMES": [
    "PLO1",
    "PLO2"
   ],
   "QUESTION_DESC": "<p data-end=\"613\" data-start=\"371\">An embedded system requires precise control over hardware sensors. Compare the use of <strong data-end=\"5665\" data-start=\"5649\">machine code</strong> and <strong data-end=\"5684\" data-start=\"5670\">C language</strong> in terms of efficiency, readability, and control.</p>\n",
   "SESSION_ID": 109,
   "TYPE": "lq",
   "key": 9382
  }
 ]
